title: SDCSC-a001: Introduction
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

Imagine walking into a room where every piece of furniture tells a story of regeneration. The air is filled with the scent of natural materials, and the furniture seems to glow with a sense of purpose. You pick out a particularly sturdy-looking chair made of wood and wool to sit upon. The chair you’ve chosen isn’t just a chair — it’s a pragmatic utopian gesture — one way to exemplify how materials can be thought of as “nutrients” in a larger resource-shed. This is the [[Oka Chair]], crafted by [[Common Object Studio|Common Objects]]. Its journey from concept to creation exemplifies circularity and provides a case study for how design thinking can evolve to work within Earth’s bounds to regenerate what surrounds.

The [[Oka Chair]] was born from a simple yet profound question: "[[How might we design a chair that gives back at every stage of its life cycle?]]" The [[Okaterra project|Okaterra]] demonstrates how local materials, traditional craftsmanship, and circular thinking can transform furniture production. Using wool from regional sheep farms practicing [[Silvopasture]], hemp cord from nearby fields, and fallen lumber from local woodlands and Baltimore city proper, each chair actively contributes to soil health, supports farmer livelihoods, and creates skilled manufacturing jobs within its bioregion. When its life cycle ends, every component can return to the earth as nutrients, leaving no waste — a perfect embodiment of circular, regenerative design principles.

